WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.000
Here's what we want to try to solve first.

00:02.000 --> 00:05.000
When you go to a new form, what do we typically want to do?

00:05.000 --> 00:08.000
We want to autofocus on the first text input.

00:08.000 --> 00:09.000
That's fairly common.

00:09.000 --> 00:13.000
But the question, of course, is how do we actually do that thing?

00:13.000 --> 00:16.000
And so typically what you might do is you might throw an autofocus on the development.

00:16.000 --> 00:21.000
That sometimes works, but then we know that with JavaScript loading and stuff, things get a little bit funky.

00:21.000 --> 00:25.000
So what we're going to learn right now is to how to actually add autofocus to forms

00:25.000 --> 00:27.000
in a way that is consistent and works.

00:27.000 --> 00:30.000
What we want is the name to be the first thing that we focus on.

00:30.000 --> 00:35.000
And so the thing about reactive references is that they work both in the data context

00:35.000 --> 00:37.000
and they work in the template context.

00:37.000 --> 00:41.000
So in other words, if we want an actual HTML element to be a reference,

00:41.000 --> 00:44.000
we just tell it what the reference is going to be called.

00:44.000 --> 00:50.000
So in this case, I usually like to preface any element that's going to be an actual element with EL.

00:50.000 --> 00:52.000
So this is kind of back to the jQuery days.

00:52.000 --> 00:54.000
We might preface it with a dollar symbol.

00:54.000 --> 00:56.000
I just prefer to keep it consistent that way.

00:56.000 --> 00:59.000
And then I'll call this like name input, for example.

00:59.000 --> 01:04.000
And the way this works is up here inside of our reactive data, though, we do need to define it, though.

01:04.000 --> 01:05.000
And how do we define that?

01:05.000 --> 01:08.000
Well, we have this element called name input.

01:08.000 --> 01:11.000
And it's going to be a reactive reference just like the reference in the template.

01:11.000 --> 01:16.000
But we can actually tell it that it's going to be an HTML input element.

01:16.000 --> 01:20.000
Or we can say it's, I think, null is the one that's typically an option.

01:20.000 --> 01:21.000
Because here's the thing.

01:21.000 --> 01:24.000
When JavaScript is loading, this thing does not exist.

01:24.000 --> 01:26.000
So it has to be able to be null.

01:26.000 --> 01:31.000
However, we do know once it's loaded, it will basically put an input element in there.

01:31.000 --> 01:35.000
And so that's why believe it or not, that's all it takes for this thing to exist.

01:35.000 --> 01:43.000
So if I log right now, log, whoops, that was a wrong name input, save it.

01:43.000 --> 01:51.000
So you'll see that if I go to the new page, you'll see that it's already fetching that input automatically.

01:51.000 --> 01:56.000
I don't have to go on-mounted, check to see if that element exists, assign it to the rough,

01:56.000 --> 01:59.000
like that's just it's there, it works.

01:59.000 --> 02:02.000
And that's kind of a little bit of the magic sprinkling that view does for us,

02:02.000 --> 02:05.000
by assigning that rough to here.

02:05.000 --> 02:07.000
So it looks to map those two things.

02:07.000 --> 02:10.000
So that's a key thing to understand.

02:10.000 --> 02:12.000
Okay, now that we have this, we can say,

02:12.000 --> 02:14.000
when everything has been mounted properly,

02:14.000 --> 02:19.000
so we'll call our on-mounted lifecycle hook, there we go.

02:19.000 --> 02:23.000
So when you have it mounted, what your initial thought is probably going to say,

02:23.000 --> 02:26.000
okay, well, I have a name input and I'm just going to run focus on it.

02:26.000 --> 02:30.000
But as we see here, TypeScript is yelling at us because, again,

02:30.000 --> 02:33.000
we've said that it might be null, it might not be defined.

02:33.000 --> 02:36.000
So what I tried to do earlier here is I was like, okay,

02:36.000 --> 02:37.000
well, we can check, right?

02:37.000 --> 02:41.000
Like, you typically would, but if it exists, then just run this thing.

02:41.000 --> 02:45.000
But it's kind of being finicky because I think it's either the way,

02:45.000 --> 02:49.000
like the HTML element is being populated, like the truthy value.

02:49.000 --> 02:54.000
So the way this actually works is you actually want to provide a question mark at it.

02:54.000 --> 02:57.000
So we want to actually unpack the value because we don't forget,

02:57.000 --> 03:00.000
element name input is a reactive reference.

03:00.000 --> 03:02.000
It's not just, and it's still reactive reference,

03:02.000 --> 03:04.000
even though it's referring to a div element.

03:04.000 --> 03:07.000
But more importantly, this value here, it could be null.

03:07.000 --> 03:10.000
So by putting the question mark here, I think this is, I want to say,

03:10.000 --> 03:11.000
this is just vanilla JavaScript.

03:11.000 --> 03:13.000
It basically, option or chaining,

03:13.000 --> 03:17.000
checks to make sure this thing is valid before doing the next thing.

03:17.000 --> 03:20.000
Although it makes me wonder now real quick,

03:20.000 --> 03:25.000
I want to test this because I, if element input dot value,

03:25.000 --> 03:27.000
would that have done it?

03:27.000 --> 03:29.000
Ah, that works too.

03:29.000 --> 03:30.000
So that's the key.

03:30.000 --> 03:32.000
So this is where the unwrapping, that's why I say,

03:32.000 --> 03:34.000
I want to people to get used to seeing that,

03:34.000 --> 03:36.000
is because it's easy to forget this to be honest.

03:36.000 --> 03:38.000
And so I was just like, oh yeah, it's definitely,

03:38.000 --> 03:39.000
it's element value.

03:39.000 --> 03:40.000
Now it's, it's dot value.

03:40.000 --> 03:41.000
This, this works.

03:41.000 --> 03:44.000
But I gotta say, there is something clean about just saying,

03:44.000 --> 03:48.000
whenever this thing is available, just, just use it.

03:48.000 --> 03:51.000
So option or chaining here is a pretty nice solution for this.

03:51.000 --> 03:54.000
Now when we refresh the page and go to new,

03:54.000 --> 03:57.000
you can see automatically focused.

